#
# throw exception — raises the named exception, which unwinds like a shell
# error to the nearest `{ ... } always { ... }` whose always part catches it
# with catch. Unhandled, it ends the script, or returns an interactive shell
# to its prompt.
#
# Written from zshcontrib(1) and from what the installed shell answers, never
# from its function files; docs/spec/functions.md has the measurements. The
# name travels in EXCEPTION. The error is an assignment to a read-only
# parameter, with its complaint sent nowhere, so nothing is printed.

typeset -g EXCEPTION="$1"
# Thrown from the always part of a try, the error has to outlive that part,
# as an error raised in the try part would.
(( TRY_BLOCK_ERROR >= 0 )) && (( TRY_BLOCK_ERROR = 1 ))
(( ${+_throw_readonly} )) || typeset -gr _throw_readonly=
{ _throw_readonly= } 2>/dev/null
